{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-302650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}{\\pi x}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-302650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}{\\pi x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)}{\\pi \\cdot (1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-302650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8cff4ac340bd6e40ac5b1fa255e0ab63c82fcfbf829f19893dbe03e69080e7ab"} {"question" "q-302650" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-302650" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-152757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-152757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-152757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8cff4ac340bd6e40ac5b1fa255e0ab63c82fcfbf829f19893dbe03e69080e7ab"} {"question" "q-152757" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/1399646