ield/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5\\pi"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-152890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-152890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/ca5e8035505f20619b6707d6d1f2d0b7b2f125a004a0dbd9d448840b0e4ffd99.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-152890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8cff4ac340bd6e40ac5b1fa255e0ab63c82fcfbf829f19893dbe03e69080e7ab"} {"question" "q-152890" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-152890" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-152890" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-339500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1))=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-339500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/f5cef3bda76e80edfa467a90823c9b6ad6aca1f8a5e8e3e06dc585258e975e0a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-339500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8cff4ac340bd6e40ac5b1fa255e0ab63c82fcfbf829f19893dbe03e69080e7ab"} {"question" "q-339500" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-339500" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-344578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}tan\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-344578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/ca5e8035505f20619b6707d6d1f2d0b7b2f125a004a0dbd9d448840b0e4ffd99.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}tan\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =tan\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-344578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8cff4ac340bd6e40ac5b1fa255e0ab63c82fcfbf829f19893dbe03e69080e7ab"} {"question" "q-344578" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-344578" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-149958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-149958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-149958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8cff4ac340bd6e40ac5b1fa255e0ab63c82fcfbf829f19893dbe03e69080e7ab"} {"question" "q-149958" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-149958" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}"] ["math" "\\infty "] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\text{DNE}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-149958" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-302690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-302690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-302690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8cff4ac340bd6e40ac5b1fa255e0ab63c82fcfbf829f19893dbe03e69080e7ab"} {"question" "q-302690" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "q-302690" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9ff0f3558ca217960d2a9f3b1793de5172c49c6a4c8443844475fca7fa9cb61"} {"question" "e-10535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\arccos(x) -\\pi}{x-\\pi}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/example-10535.json" "file_sha256" "89d86a4c8e1e0a6a01e17336950970a6c2bb822121ced029d8592aa7055ebf7a"} {"question" "e-10535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\arccos(x){:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)-\\pi }{x-\\pi }) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)-\\pi }{0-\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi )}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/example-10535.json" "file_sha256" "89d86a4c8e1e0a6a01e17336950970a6c2bb822121ced029d8592aa7055ebf7a"} {"question" "e-10536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/example-10536.json" "file_sha256" "b799aa187a209ab9ecb84cd0a1696d3e217b2ef736ba4cc407421da70b5d0e56"} {"question" "e-10536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's sketch the graph of $y = \\arccos(x-1){:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/4ccf7e79e19f49027f615a075e655139e6926b3ba30fe35d63078fd7917d8869.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x-1)$ is $x \\in [0,2].$ This means that $\\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/example-10536.json" "file_sha256" "b799aa187a209ab9ecb84cd0a1696d3e217b2ef736ba4cc407421da70b5d0e56"} {"question" "e-10537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty}\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2}{3}\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right)\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/example-10537.json" "file_sha256" "1bcfc819b215b6176d9540215b724f9635a9895e816902ac805ae58b81493228"} {"question" "e-10537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right){:}$  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/06d01e3eb47f69e5172e0ba3c99708a05c79b12a48764fe29c50e94f93b73dd1.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(2x+1 \\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty,$ the graph tends to $y={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/example-10537.json" "file_sha256" "1bcfc819b215b6176d9540215b724f9635a9895e816902ac805ae58b81493228"}] "usage" {"e-10537" [] "e-10536" [] "e-10535" [] "q-149958" []} "basis" 1080 "retractions" [[17592186184460 :question/problem "What is $lim_(x → - 1/2)\\tan (\\frac{1}{2}\\arccos (x))$?"] [17592186183865 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\arccos(x) -\\pi}{x-\\pi}$."] [17592186183865 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arccos(x){:}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3811/Source/Images/a466fdfbf193e117a9c5e416ee47ffff.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1]$.\n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)((\\arccos (x) - π)/(x - π)) &= (\\arccos (0) - π)/(0 - π) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{π}{2} - π))/((- π)) \\\\\n&= ((-\\frac{π}{2}))/((- π)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186183866 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)$?"] [17592186183866 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\arccos(x-1){:}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3811/Source/Images/0c8e05c838e67b1eaca00925fab12e20.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x-1)$ is $x \\in [0,2]$. This means that $\\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined."] [17592186183867 :question/problem "Compute the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right)\\right)$."] [17592186183867 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny =\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right){:}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3811/Source/Images/3e8fb5c89782322156fbf09f33393408.png)\n\nThe domain of\n\n$$\n\\arctan \\left(2x+1 \\right)\n$$\n\nis $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty$, the graph tends to\n\n$$\ny={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → ∞)\\cos (\\frac{2}{3}\\arctan (2x + 1)) &= \\cos (\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot (\\frac{π}{2})) \\\\\n&= \\cos (\\frac{π}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186181209 17592186181210 17592186181211 17592186181212 17592186181213] "immutable_fields" [[17592186181208 :answer-field/correct] [17592186181208 :answer-field/id] [17592186181208 :answer-field/key] [17592186181208 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
